  "opennyai_summary": {
    "PREAMBLE": "Virender Kumar vs The State (Da) on 23 September, 2016\n1\n In The Court Of Sh. Rakesh Pandit,\n Asj-01, New Delhi District, Patiala House Courts,\n New Delhi\nCa No. 78/16\nVirender Kumar\nS/o Sh. G. P Gupta\nM/s Aggarwal Super Store,\nC-131A, Thana Road, Gali No. 5,\nBhajan Pura, Delhi. ....Appellant\n Vs.\nThe State (Da) .... Respondent\n Date of receiving of Appeal : 04.07.2016\nDate of arguments : 24.08.2016\n Date of judgment : 23.09.2016\nJudgment\n1 By this judgment I will dispose of appeal u/sec.374 Cr.PC. filed on behalf of convict Virender Kumar against the judgment dated 21.03.2013 and order on sentence dated 28.03.2013 passed by Sh. Balwant Rai Bansal, Ld. ACMM-II, Patiala House Court, New Delhi.\n",
    "facts": "The said Dal Arhar was purchased for the purposes of analysis under PFA Act.\n3 As the sample was failed, prosecution was launched by Food Inspector by filing complaint dated 16.04.2003 with the court.\nHe has not exercised his right u/sec.13(2) PFA Act and get the sample analyzed by CFL.\nVirender Kumar vs The State (Da) on 23 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/168919922/\n1 4\nCharge was framed against accused u/sec.2(ia) (a) (j)\n(m) read with sec.7 of PFA Act, 1954 punishable u/sec.16(1A) of PFA Act.\n",
    "ANALYSIS": "So, even if a traces of colour is found in the Dal, the same can be termed as adulterated.\nSo, there is no need for giving percentage of colour by the scientist i.e. PA or CFL.\nMoreover, the non- compliance falls under the violation of adulteration and not misbranding.\nVirender Kumar vs The State (Da) on 23 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/168919922/\n2 11\nHowever, it is not the law that the FI shall spell out on which surfaces the sample commodity had touched while sampling.\nHowever, if he chooses to spell out the implements/surfaces with which the sample commodity came into contact/touch, the same must be proved as clean and dry.\n15 In the present case, the PW2/FI Suniti Kumar Gupta had stated in his cross-examination dated 24.02.2009 that the Dal was weighed after putting in a brown paper.\nNowhere this witness or any other witness by the prosecution had proved on record that this brown paper was clean and free from colour and could not have added any colour to the Dal.\n16 So, in these circumstances, there is violation of Rule 14 of PFA Rules and thus the benefit goes in favour of appellant.\n",
    "decision": "So, in these circumstances, the judgment and order on sentence of the trial court is reversed.\nAccused Virender Kumar is acquitted of the charges u/sec.16(1A) read with sec.7 of PFA Act.\nAccepted for six months.\nAccused Virender Kumar is acquitted of the charges u/sec.16(1A) read with sec.7 of PFA Act.\n"
  },
